// `idparse --fingerprints`: one line per function, `<fingerprint>\t<name>\t
// <file>:<line>`, instead of a build. cfp already holds every function's
// fingerprint, once check_unique() has run (../scan/pass/check.id) -- this is a
// reader of that, not a second walk of the tree.

// One line per fingerprint, which is why the walk is over `cfp` and not `prog`:
// a chained function is meant to contribute one entry per segment, and then the
// two lists differ in length. The cases pin the count and the indices used, so
// a mismatch fails here rather than quietly printing a report whose line count
// no longer matches what it describes.
print_fingerprints() {
  int i = 0;
  while(i < len((import cfp))) {
    print_fp_line(i);
    i = i + 1;
  }
} return void;
given lz_o ():() then calls print_fp_line:[2]
given lz_o ():() then calls print_fp_line:(0) then calls print_fp_line:(1)

// `i` indexes the fingerprints; the function it belongs to is resolved by
// fname_at (../scan/more/report/report.id), the checker's one place for that
// mapping, rather than by indexing `prog` with a fingerprint's index here.
print_fp_line(int i) {
  string name = fname_at(i);
  string line = fp_line(name, (import cfp)[i]);
  print(line);
} return void;

// file:line, the same two facts a diagnostic's prefix names (../../names/
// limits/report/more/prefix.id), without the ": error: " a diagnostic adds.
fp_line(string name, string fp) {
  string tab = chr(9);
  string ret_s = fp + tab + name + tab + func_file(name) + ":" + func_line(name);
} return string ret_s;
